= Background
This section presents relevant research and frameworks to establish an overview of research on therapist interfaces for rehabilitation through exergames. This chapter first establishes our research ground in exergames & automation of rehabilitation work to show existing technology use and automation frameworks. Secondly, it sets entanglement as the theoretical lens with the agential cut and co-performance to define agency and the sense of agency. Finally, it reviews existing therapists interfaces for exergames-based rehabilitation.

== Exergames & Automation in Rehabilitation
Exergames, a fusion of "exercise" and "video games", are video games that promote physical activity and cognitive engagement through gamified tasks @ohyou2010. Although not exclusively, they are often classified under a broader category "Serious Games", which refers to digital games designed primarily for purposes beyond entertainments, such as education, training, and health interventions @Maggio2025. The researches also differentiate between commercial off-the-shelf systems and custom systems used for rehabilitation purposes @Allegue2022. The meta-analyses show exergames systems specifically designed for rehabilitation tend to be more effective than conventional rehabilitation, whereas re-purposed commercial off-the-shelf systems often yield mixed or neutral results, in part because their interaction mechanics and feedback are not tightly aligned with therapeutic goals @Maier2019. Moreover, exergames have shown to support neuroplasticity by enabling high repetition and high intensity task-oriented activities that are challenging enough but not frustrating resulting in increased motivation and engagement @Maier2019 @Allegue2022 @Shah2022. 

A wide range of technologies have been explored to implement exergames for rehabilitation. Non-immersive systems based on Xbox Kinect, Nintendo Wii, PlayStation, robot-assisted serious games, or similar motion-sensing consoles are widely studied and have shown be be clinically provide effective rehabilitation compared to conventional rehabilitation @Cheok2015 @Santos2025 @Doumas2025. Similarly, XR-based exergames, which are more immersive in nature, have been used for motor and cognitive rehabilitation after stroke and other neurological conditions, with studies reporting improvements in motor functions, spatial attention, and improved engagement compared to conventional exercises @Fordell2016 @Faria2016. Beside providing a gamified exercise and its effectiveness in clinical treatment, exergames can also be viewed as interactive systems that generate performance data which can be used to monitor progress and support clinical-decision making. Exergames inherently generate fine-grained performance data (such as reaction times, errors, speed) that can be used to adapt game logics in real-time or to monitor progress and support clinical decision-making process across sessions @Ismail2021. Additionally, through AI and Machine Learning approaches for analyzing sensors, game data and performance data, *they can* aid how interfaces, algorithms and workflows can be designed to support personalized rehabilitation alongside providing gamified exercise experience @AttohMensah2025. Exergames, thus, have higher potential of accurately providing and utilizing data-centric monitoring during therapy sessions and create adaptable and fun experiences to fit individual needs allowing patients to participate in interactive and adaptive exercises @Rahin2025. 

Besides in-game data, automation is increasingly transforming rehabilitation work, with many work processes now being carried out either partially or fully by automated systems @TurmoVidal2025 @Yu2026. These automated data-driven systems support personalized rehabilitation, and reduce therapists workload while preserving the human aspects and judgement. Exergame platforms with recommendation systems automatically select and plan personalized exercises based on performance histories, biofeedback, and other data points, shifting agency of clinical decision-making from therapist to algorithms @Ismail2021 @Zhao2020. These studies demonstrate that exergames can be implemented and are being explored in varied technologies *including across* different rehabilitation settings while automation in the these systems are enhancing the work process to provide personalized rehabilitation. However, treating personalization as a purely technical optimization and user experience problem would be short-sighted without considering patient preferences, motivational factors and meaningful therapeutic goals.    
